    {
      "heading": "Dwell time and skipped posts",
      "body": "Clicks and reactions are rare for people who mostly read, so LinkedIn also measures dwell time, which on the feed starts when at least half of a post is visible [1][2]. Posts viewed only briefly with no action count as skipped, and a P(skip) model now lowers every post's score in proportion to its predicted chance of being skipped [3]. LinkedIn's Help Center adds that the feed mixes your network's posts with recommended content labeled Trending, Recommended for you or Popular [4]. See also [[Watch time]].",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/blog/engineering/feed/understanding-feed-dwell-time",
          "title": "Understanding dwell time to improve LinkedIn feed ranking (LinkedIn Engineering, May 2020)",
          "quote": "Click and viral actions can be rare, especially for passive consumers of the feed."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/blog/engineering/feed/understanding-feed-dwell-time",
          "title": "Understanding dwell time to improve LinkedIn feed ranking (LinkedIn Engineering, May 2020)",
